Achilles Tendinopathy
Musculoskeletal & Pain
Posterior heel pain, Focal tendon thickening, Pain on single-leg heel raise

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-001
Reason for Visit: Acute incapacitating posterior heel pain preventing independent ambulation and weight-bearing.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated sudden inability to weight-bear coupled with severe focal pain. Urgent physical assessment was required to exclude acute tendon rupture, manage acute pain crisis, and implement immediate mobility aids to prevent further functional and physiological deterioration before the next available in-hours consultation.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My heel suddenly flared up today, and it feels like a knife when I put any weight on it."
History of Present Illness: Gradual onset of localized pain over the posterior heel exacerbated by weight-bearing activities over the past three weeks. Condition acutely deteriorated 12 hours prior to presentation with severe exacerbation of pain preventing standard ambulation. Timeline: Initial mild morning stiffness in the ankle improving with movement; progressed to constant dull ache; evolved today into acute sharp pain on any ankle dorsiflexion. Patient reports an antalgic gait during the push-off phase of walking prior to becoming completely non-weight-bearing today.
Functional Status: Temporarily homebound, requires support for indoor mobilization.
Social/Safety Concerns: Fall risk secondary to impaired mobility and severe antalgic gait.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 142/88 | HR: 94 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 84 Kg
General: Alert, in moderate distress when moving the affected lower limb.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular and respiratory systems unremarkable. Lower limb exam reveals palpable thickening and nodularity of the Achilles tendon 4 cm proximal to the calcaneal insertion. Exquisite focal tenderness on palpation of the mid-portion of the tendon. Pain is significantly reproduced by passive dorsiflexion. Patient exhibits marked pain on attempted single-leg heel raise. No palpable gap in the tendon.
Environmental Observations: Polished floorboards present a slip risk while mobilizing with an altered gait.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular analgesia prescribed. Paracetamol independently utilized with minimal effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute exacerbation of Achilles Tendinopathy - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Achilles Tendon Rupture - Ruled out due to intact plantarflexion against gravity and absence of a palpable tendon gap.

Calcaneal Stress Fracture - Ruled out due to lack of bony tenderness on calcaneal squeeze test.

Retrocalcaneal Bursitis - Ruled out due to maximal tenderness located proximal to the insertion site, rather than at the bursal anatomic location.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute on chronic severe localized posterior heel pain resulting in a sudden inability to weight-bear. Clinical examination confirmed focal tendon thickening, exquisite mid-substance tenderness, and severe pain on functional loading, consistent with an acute inflammatory flare of Achilles tendinopathy without complete rupture. Given the severe functional impairment, significant pain limiting mobility, and the immediate fall risk in the home environment, delaying assessment until the next in-hours period was clinically unsafe. Urgent after-hours assessment was definitively required to exclude surgical emergencies, administer appropriate potent analgesia, and establish an immediate safe mobilization strategy to prevent further injury and functional decline.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed oral NSAIDs (Ibuprofen 400mg TDS with food) for 5 days. Recommended ultrasound imaging of the ankle in-hours if symptoms do not improve.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Apply ice packs to the heel for 15 minutes after any necessary walking. Consequence: Rapid reduction in throbbing pain and local swelling (negative reinforcement).
Action: Perform gentle seated calf stretches. Consequence: Noticeable reduction in morning stiffness, granting easier initial steps out of bed (positive reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Physiotherapy referral recommended for load management.
DME/Safety (Optional): Provided temporary use of a single crutch to offload the affected side.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3-5 days for ongoing load management and imaging referral if refractory.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if there is a sudden distinct 'pop' sensation in the heel, an inability to push the foot down at all, or the development of a fever with a hot, red, swollen calf.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive based on clinical examination; imaging may be required if recovery is atypical. Conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Tendinopathy flares typically improve with acute load reduction and analgesia over 3-7 days, but full tendon remodeling requires prolonged rehabilitation.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency care if you completely lose the ability to point your toes downward or if you experience a sudden tearing sensation in the back of the calf.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: NSAIDs are provided to modulate the acute inflammatory pain response, allowing for basic protected mobility. Crutch use prevents further microtrauma to the tendon mid-substance.
Actionable Steps: Continue the ice application after movement to suppress pain. Utilize the crutch consistently to protect the healing tissue and experience less pain during essential daily tasks.

